// 059 — Identity operator (is vs ==) — DESIGN NOTE, what not to do
//
// Python has two ways to ask "are these the same?":
//   a == b   → value equality (calls __eq__)
//   a is b   → identity (same object in memory)
// Most of the time they give the same answer. Occasionally they don't,
// and the bugs are subtle: `300 == 300` is True, `300 is 300` might be
// False depending on small-int caching. JavaScript has three flavors
// (==, ===, Object.is), each with different coercion rules.
//
// AiLang has one equality: EqualTo. It compares values. There is no
// identity operator — if two things share an address, `EqualTo(a, b)`
// returns 1. If they carry the same integer, `EqualTo(a, b)` returns
// 1. The distinction doesn't exist in the language because it's
// almost never what you want to ask, and when it IS what you want,
// comparing addresses directly is already trivial.
SubRoutine.Main {
    a = 300
    b = 300
    PrintMessage("EqualTo(a, b): ")
    PrintNumber(EqualTo(a, b))
    PrintMessage("\n")

    // Two strings with the same contents — live at different
    // addresses, but EqualTo on their addresses still makes sense for
    // pointer-equality semantics. For value equality of strings, call
    // a string compare instead; don't try to overload EqualTo.
}
RunTask(Main)
